#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --script
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.11"
# dependencies = [
#     "httpx>=0.27",
#     "livekit>=1.1,<2",
#     "pyyaml>=6",
#     "capabilities-contract==0.6.0",
# ]
# ///
"""callvatest — agent-first CLI that regression-tests a deployed CallVA voice agent.

This tool is designed to be invoked by agents. Run `callvatest help` to load the
full usage contract on demand — do not rely on memorised knowledge of its surface.

It joins a real LiveKit room as a headless participant through the ordinary CallVA
WebRTC connect flow, drives the conversation over text, and then asserts what the
deployed agent actually did: the transcript it produced, the tools it called, the
arguments it generated, the results it answered from, and the fields the platform
stored after the call. There is no test-only runtime path — the agent under test
loads its production configuration and calls its production tools, so a passing
run is evidence about production behaviour.

Placing a call costs money and creates a real call record. `run`, `suite`,
`probe`, `explore`, and `session start` are therefore write verbs: a connection
with "allow_write": false refuses them, and no flag lifts that gate.

CONNECTIONS
  Configuration requires an explicit connections registry, even for one
  connection. Its values resolve by the cascade (first non-empty wins):
    1. flags          --api-url (non-secret only; a secret never has a flag)
    2. project env    .env.local then .env, walking up from $CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR (else cwd)
    3. user config    $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/callvatest/credentials.env
    4. process env    CALLVA_API_KEY / CALLVA_API_URL
  The key is only ever named, never passed on argv.
    CALLVA_API_KEY=<CallVA API key>        secret — the tenant that owns the agent
    CALLVA_API_URL=<base URL>              optional; default
                                           https://api.callva.one/api/v1

  A project that tests several CallVA tenants declares them as
  connections, kept where "Where connections live" below says:
    {
      "default": "<id>",
      "connections": {
        "<id>": {
          "api_url": "https://api.example.com/api/v1",
          "secret_env": "CALLVA_<ID>_API_KEY",
          "allow_write": true,
          "test_call_field": "test_call"
        }
      }
    }
  "secret_env" names the environment variable holding the key; naming a variable
  the project already defines keeps one home for the secret. "allow_write": false
  makes a connection observation-only — useful for pointing at a live tenant you
  must not place calls into.

  "test_call_field" names a boolean custom field on the tenant's calls, set to
  true on every call this harness places. The platform stores a harness call
  exactly like a caller's, so without it a tenant's own reporting counts the
  testing as traffic. Declare it only where that field exists — `doctor` proves
  it does. Absent, nothing is written.

  Selection: --connection <id>. Unflagged, the registry's "default" pointer
  applies, else the sole entry; several entries without a pointer is refused.

  Outside any project, connections and doctor are machine reads: they use the
  machine's own connections read-only, unless a grant switched one off; every
  other verb that needs a connection is refused there.

JUDGES
  A `semantic` assertion is settled by a judge model. A judge is this harness's
  own configuration rather than a CallVA tenant, so the roster is the "judges"
  setting, written like any other setting record:

    capabilities set callvatest setting judges '{
      "default": "haiku",
      "connections": {
        "haiku":   {"provider": "anthropic",
                    "model": "claude-haiku-4-5-20251001",
                    "secret_env": "ANTHROPIC_API_KEY"},
        "gpt-oss": {"provider": "cerebras", "model": "gpt-oss-120b",
                    "secret_env": "CEREBRAS_API_KEY",
                    "reasoning_effort": "low"},
        "terra":   {"provider": "azure", "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
                    "endpoint": "my-resource.openai.azure.com",
                    "api_version": "2025-04-01-preview",
                    "secret_env": "AZURE_OPENAI_API_KEY"}
      }
    }'

  It resolves project-then-global like every setting, so a machine-wide roster
  is declared once and a project overrides it by declaring its own.
  `callvatest connections` reports every judge it resolved, its model, the
  address it composed, and where its key came from, without calling anything.

  Providers are "anthropic", "cerebras" and "azure"; the model is named per
  judge, so a backend is switched in the roster or per invocation with
  --judge <id>, never in a scenario. Keys resolve by the same cascade as the API
  key and are never passed on argv. A judge is resolved only when a scenario
  actually declares a `semantic` claim.

  An azure model is a deployment inside one resource, so an azure judge names
  its own "endpoint" and the model is the deployment name; "api_version" is
  optional and defaults to a version this harness pins. "reasoning_effort" is
  optional on the providers that read it, and refused on the ones that do not.

  A steady verdict is asked for by constraint rather than by sampling: the judge
  is given the tool results as ground truth and must answer with a two-field
  JSON object, and a reply it cannot be parsed from fails the assertion instead
  of passing it. Temperature 0 is sent where the provider takes it, and the
  newest Azure families refuse every value but their own. `fixtures` is how a
  judge is shown to be steady on the claims this project actually asserts.

  A judge runs after the call has ended, so its accuracy decides the choice and
  its latency does not.

SCENARIOS
  Project-owned CallVA artefacts live under `callva/` in the project, by kind.
  Scenarios are one kind:

    <project>/callva/tests/<agent>/<language>/<scenario>.yaml

  `run` and `suite` walk up from the working directory to find `callva/tests`.
  --path overrides the search entirely.

  A scenario is YAML:

    id: earliest-slot
    agent: <agent-label>          # ids label, or an agent uuid
    language: en
    turns:
      - say: "I would like to book an appointment."
        pause_after_reply: 2       # optional extra silence before the next turn
        expect:
          tools_not_called: ["Availability Lookup"]
          reply_not_matches: "(?i)cannot help"
      - say: "Tell me the earliest time you have."
        expect:
          tool_called: "Availability Lookup"
          tool_args:
            service_id: "<service-id>"
          semantic: "The agent offers the earliest slot from the result."
    expect:                       # evaluated once the call has settled
      audio_observed: true
      assistant_turn_after_tool: true
      no_internal_ids: true
      tool_call_count:
        "Availability Lookup": 1
      post_call:
        status: completed

  A tool is named by a case-insensitive substring of its automation name, or by
  an automation uuid. Times, dates, and slots are never pinned as literals — they
  are asserted against the tool result the agent actually received.

  `post_call` reads the call record the platform stored once the call settled:
  its own outcome fields, and any custom field the tenant defines on a call.
  Each named field is compared as a string, so a scenario asserts the tenant's
  own schema without this harness knowing it.

  `semantic` states one claim in prose and hands it to a judge model together
  with the tool results of that turn and what the agent said; a call-level
  `semantic` is given the whole transcript and every run. Use it where the
  medium defeats matching in code — an agent speaks "kell kümme", never 10:00,
  and no regular expression will call that the same time. Everything structured
  (which tool ran, its arguments, counts, leaked ids) stays deterministic: code
  is exact, free, and never flickers. A judge that cannot answer fails the
  check; it never passes it.

COMMANDS
  probe [--agent <id|label>] [--say TEXT ...] [--turns N]
        One diagnostic call with no assertions: connect, join, drive the turns
        given by --say, then print everything observed — transcript with
        timings, agent state changes, audio frames, the resolved call id, tool
        runs with their arguments, and tool results. This is the first thing to
        run against a new agent, and the first thing to run when a scenario
        fails for reasons the report does not explain.

  run <scenario> [--agent <id|label>] [--report FILE]
        Execute one scenario and print its verdict. Exit 1 when it fails.

  suite [<path>] [--agent <id|label>] [--jobs N] [--report FILE]
        Execute every scenario under a path. Exit 1 if any fails. --jobs runs N
        calls at once, and reports keep scenario order however the runs finish.
        A scenario that collapses is counted as a failure and named, and the
        rest of the suite continues.

  fixtures [<path>] [--judge <id>] [--all-judges] [--report FILE]
        Judge the judge, without placing a call. Each fixture case freezes tool
        results, what an agent said, a claim, and the verdict a competent judge
        must reach; the run reports every disagreement. Exit 1 when any case
        disagrees. Fixtures live in <project>/callva/judge/:

          id: et-times
          cases:
            - claim: "Every time the agent speaks appears in the tool result."
              results: ['{"earliest": "reede, 4. september 09:00"}']
              said: "Kõige varasem vaba aeg on reedel kell üheksa."
              expect: true

        Half the cases must expect false, or the run cannot tell a judge that
        reads from one that agrees with everything. --all-judges runs the whole
        set past every declared judge, which is how backends are compared on
        material that matters rather than on a benchmark.

        `known_gap: true` marks a case every judge already fails — a measured
        limit rather than a regression. It still runs and is still reported, but
        it does not fail the run, because a set that is permanently red gates
        nothing; the day a judge starts getting it right is reported too.

  doctor
        Per connection: prove the key resolves and reaches the API, and that the
        LiveKit RTC stack imports. Names the agents the key can see, proves a
        declared "test_call_field" exists on the tenant's calls, and asks each
        declared judge to settle a trivial claim.

SESSIONS
  A scenario is written before the call; a session is decided during it. One
  call is held open across separate invocations, so the next line is chosen
  after reading the reply to the last one — which is how a person, or an agent
  acting as one, actually talks.

  session start [--agent <id|label>] [--text]
        Place a call and leave it open. Billable, exactly as `run` is: this is
        the write verb of the four. It returns
        {"session","room","agent","mode","log","greeting","turns"} — the id
        every other session verb takes, and the greeting already spoken.

  session say <id> "<line>" [--timeout <s>]
        Send one caller line and wait for the agent to finish answering it.
        Returns {"session","turn","reply","elapsed"}: `reply` is what the agent
        said in that turn alone. A turn that times out is reported as an error
        and leaves the call up, so the next line — or `end` — still works.

  session end <id>
        Hang up and report. Returns the same object `run` produces —
        transcript, events, tool runs with their arguments, the resolved call
        id, and the post-call record once it settles — with "verdict":
        "observed", because a session asserts nothing.

  session list
        Every session this machine holds: id, agent, room, mode, turns so far,
        age, whether its holder is alive, and whether it has ended.

  The call lives in a holder process that `start` leaves behind, and each event
  is written to <session>/log.jsonl the moment it happens. So if the call drops
  or the holder dies, nothing is lost: `say` then refuses by naming the log, and
  `end` still reports from it plus whatever the platform can still be asked.

EXPLORING
  A scenario says every caller line in advance, so it can gate: red means the
  agent changed. An exploration says only where the call must go and lets a
  model play the caller, so it can find: the wording is invented on the spot,
  a persona makes the caller unclear, wandering or insistent, and the run
  reports what it saw instead of passing or failing.

  explore <exploration> [--persona <id>] [--driver <id>] [--agent <id|label>]
          [--report FILE]
        Let the driver model choose every caller line until every stage is
        reached, a budget is spent, or the caller gives up. Billable, exactly
        as `run` is, and it may place more than one call: a call that ends
        under the caller before the stages are done is followed by another
        while the budget allows. Prints the exploratory report; --json for the
        whole record. Exit 0 whatever was found; 5 only when the driver itself
        could not answer, which is a fault of the run and not a finding.

  Explorations, personas, the driver and the budget are one setting,
  "explore", declared beside "judges" and resolved the same way, project then
  global:

    capabilities set callvatest setting explore '{
      "driver": "gpt-oss",
      "budget": {"turns": 12, "calls": 1},
      "personas": {
        "mumbler": "You speak in fragments, trail off mid-sentence, and about
                    half the time answer a different question than the one
                    you were asked.",
        "insister": "You want the earliest possible slot and push back on
                     every alternative twice before accepting anything."
      },
      "explorations": {
        "books-any-afternoon": {
          "agent": "<agent-label>",
          "language": "en",
          "intent": "Book a consultation next week, any afternoon, as a
                     returning caller.",
          "stages": ["state the reason for calling",
                     "give a preferred day and time when asked",
                     "confirm the details the agent reads back",
                     "say goodbye"],
          "persona": "mumbler"
        }
      }
    }'

  "driver" names an entry of the judges roster — the same providers, models
  and keys, so no new account or key is needed; gpt-oss-120b on Cerebras is a
  fitting one. --driver <id> switches it for one run. The driver answers while
  the agent waits, so a fast model matters here where it does not for a judge.
  A setting that names no driver is refused; nothing falls back to the judges
  default.

  "budget" is counted in what this harness can count. "turns" is the most
  caller lines one run may send, across every call it places; "calls" is the
  most calls it may place. Both are required, and the run stops at whichever
  is spent first. The whole-call --budget in seconds still bounds each call
  on its own. The price of a call is the tenant's to know, never this
  harness's to guess, so no budget is money.

  An exploration's "intent" is the caller's purpose and the facts they hold;
  "stages" are the points the call must pass through, in order. The driver
  invents the wording inside that direction and says which stages the
  conversation has completed. "agent" and "language" are optional and read as
  a scenario's are; "persona" names the persona this exploration plays by
  default, and --persona <id> overrides it for one run.

  "personas" are prose, one paragraph each, describing how a caller speaks
  and behaves; a persona changes nothing about what the caller wants. They
  are the project's own: this harness ships none and plays no particular
  caller.

  The report is its own shape, "kind": "exploration": the intent, each stage
  with the call and turn that reached it, the persona, the driver, the budget
  and what was spent of it, what stopped the run, one record per call placed
  — room, call id, transcript, events, tool runs and post-call fields, what a
  `run` report holds without its checks — and the driver's notes: one
  sentence per turn where the agent did something a caller would find wrong,
  odd or unhelpful. The verdict is one of:
    explored   every stage was reached
    stalled    a budget was spent first — turns or calls
    dropped    the caller gave up first: the driver ended the call with
               stages still to go
    aborted    the driver could not answer; nothing about the agent is claimed
  None of these is pass or fail, and none gates anything: a finding is turned
  into a scenario, and the scenario is what gates.

  Contract verbs: help, doctor, connections, stub, manifest --json,
  guide [<topic>], ids list|get|set|rm, refs, inventory [--network].

GLOBAL OPTIONS
  --connection <id>   select a registry connection
  --judge <id>        select a judge for this run's semantic assertions
  --text              run the call over text alone: the room carries no audio,
                      the agent never synthesizes speech, and a turn ends when
                      its text is complete. Several times faster and cheaper,
                      and it proves nothing about the speech path — checks that
                      need audio report as skipped. A scenario always describes
                      the spoken call; only this flag downgrades a run.
  --key-env <KEY>     resolve the API key from a different env/cred KEY
  --api-url <URL>     override the base URL for this invocation
  --json              machine-readable output where a verb has a prose default
  --timeout <S>       per-turn timeout in seconds (default 45)
  --budget <S>        whole-call budget in seconds (default 300)
  --trace             write the run's own instrument to stderr: every room
                      lifecycle event, each send and its outcome, and the SDK's
                      internals, on one clock. Reach for it when a run times out
                      and the report cannot say why. Output on stdout is
                      unaffected, so a report stays machine-readable.

EXIT CODES
  0 ok · 1 assertion failed · 2 credentials · 3 not found · 4 policy
  5 upstream/transport · 6 room shared with another caller, or invalid input
  7 timeout waiting on the agent
"""

